      "writtenOn": "2026-04-09T16:45:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Can we reuse the host\u0027s iommu_iotlb_gather API (for instance, the above `freelist` passed to `dma_pte_clear_level()`) instead of adding all this ad-hoc logic?",
      "revId": "f61cd346baefda5f6e40c03dde01678520eaf417",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3219b3f4_8acb2ffc",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1140,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-09T18:17:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "From my understanding, the iommu_iotlb_gather API doesn\u0027t do much. the freelist is to free the pages after the unmap and holds on to the pages until caches are invalidated. For deferred flushing, intel iommu doesn\u0027t keep track of the ranges rather does a flush all with `intel_flush_iotlb_all`. I was kind of optimizing it a bit. I did not systematically gather numbers to measure performance, but doing a flush all seemed a bit big hammer to me and hence did an easy optimization.",
      "parentUuid": "6511cbe2_e1fadeb4",
      "revId": "f61cd346baefda5f6e40c03dde01678520eaf417",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a0b6b957_7f4adbdf",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1140,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-09T18:51:45Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e For deferred flushing, intel iommu doesn\u0027t keep track of the ranges rather does a flush all with intel_flush_iotlb_all.\n\nAh indeed, now I recall that.\n\nSo, we are trying to be smarter than the upstream IOMMU driver and are deliberately making our code more complicated without even measuring if it\u0027s worth it? Whereas maybe there were good reasons why the upstream driver chose not to use page-selective flushes in this case (not even this single min..max one)?",
      "parentUuid": "3219b3f4_8acb2ffc",
      "revId": "f61cd346baefda5f6e40c03dde01678520eaf417",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9b915631_3fcfe24d",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1140,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-09T19:35:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The reason I chose to do this was, we might be flushing more often than host driver because we flush on tlb_flush as well. Interleaving unmap with donation will cause these flushes in a deferred flush scenario. \n\nI guess page selective was not chosen because generic IOMMU driver always calls a flush_all and probably do not have the mechanism to do selective flushing from the generic driver side. But I do not have concrete evidence and will look at the git log to verify.\n\nIf I remove the min and max and do global flushing based on flush_pending flag, it will be same as host driver. I can change to that if that feels better to you. I feel this optimization is not hurting and I can take more numbers with and without this fix to verify if it does give some boost",
      "parentUuid": "a0b6b957_7f4adbdf",
      "revId": "f61cd346baefda5f6e40c03dde01678520eaf417",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9d00bb6e_6d73244b",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1140,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-09T22:23:52Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m wondering what are the actual typical sizes of these min..max ranges. I imagine that, since DMA allocations can be arbitrarily sparse, these ranges may be so huge that there may be virtually no benefit compared to global flushes (or perhaps it may be even slower than global flushes?)\n\n\u003e probably do not have the mechanism to do selective flushing from the generic driver side.\n\nSurely it does have a mechanism for that, it uses it e.g. normally during DMA unmap in strict mode? `iommu_iotlb_sync()`?",
      "parentUuid": "9b915631_3fcfe24d",
      "revId": "f61cd346baefda5f6e40c03dde01678520eaf417",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2da1551e_c9560485",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1140,
      "author": {
        "id": 3702829
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-09T22:29:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e Surely it does have a mechanism for that, it uses it e.g. normally during DMA unmap in strict mode? iommu_iotlb_sync()?\n\nI meant the generic code  do not use the iommu_tlb_sync() callback on deferred mode. If I understand correctly, when the flush queue is full or after time out, it calls the flush_all callback, It uses `iommu_iotlb_sync()` callback only with strict mode I think.",
      "parentUuid": "9d00bb6e_6d73244b",
      "revId": "f61cd346baefda5f6e40c03dde01678520eaf417",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6d3729c5_df4ea48c",
        "filename": "drivers/iommu/intel/iommu.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 1140,
      "author": {
        "id": 2022314
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-04-09T23:04:21Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, obviously that\u0027s what it does, the question is _why_ it was implemented that way. :) i.e. why it doesn\u0027t try to take advantage of page-selective flushes.\n\nWell, that might be not hard to speculate: there may be an arbitrarily high number of those pending unmapped-but-not-flushed ranges accumulated, so remembering them all and then submitting an arbitrarily high number of flush descriptors to flush them all seems quite dubious; and using this min-max trick doesn\u0027t seem unequivocally advantageous either, since those ranges are arbitrarily sparse.",
      "parentUuid": "2da1551e_c9560485",
      "revId": "f61cd346baefda5f6e40c03dde01678520eaf417",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
